  "text": "The rain had started an hour ago, a soft, persistent drizzle that blurred the city lights into smeared watercolors against the floor-to-ceiling windows of the penthouse office. Elias stood by the glass, watching the droplets race each other down the pane, his reflection ghostly and pale in the darkened room. He was waiting for Thomas. They had agreed to meet here, away from the prying eyes of the junior developers and the constant hum of the server farm below. The Meridian Project was their life’s work, a culmination of years of sleepless nights, failed prototypes, and whispered arguments in the breakroom. It was supposed to be a triumph, a beacon of security in a chaotic digital landscape. But lately, the weight of it had become suffocating. Elias checked his watch for the tenth time. Thomas was late. This was unusual. Thomas was always early, always prepared, his mind a whirlwind of logic and precision. Where was he? The elevator dinged, a sharp sound that cut through the ambient hum of the building’s HVAC system. Elias turned, his heart giving a small, hopeful jump. The doors slid open, revealing not Thomas, but a corridor of empty, dimly lit hallway. No one stepped out. The doors closed with a soft hiss, leaving Elias alone with his reflection and the rain. He let out a breath he didn’t realize he’d been holding. Maybe Thomas was stuck in traffic. Maybe he was running a final diagnostic from his home office. Elias turned back to the window, the city sprawling out below him, a vast, indifferent machine. He thought about the board meeting tomorrow, the investors who expected miracles, the competitors who were circling like sharks. He thought about the code, the elegant, fragile architecture they had built. It was perfect, or so they believed. But perfection was a dangerous illusion. He reached into his pocket and pulled out his phone, the screen glowing with a single, unread notification. He hesitated, then tapped it. The message was from an unknown number, just three words: \"It’s not safe.\" Elias stared at the screen, the words burning into his retinas. The rain outside intensified, the sound growing louder, more urgent, as if the sky itself was trying to warn him of the storm that was about to break."
