  "rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and, in the text field, the passage of about 250 words that came immediately BEFORE the current text in its story.",
  "text": "The silence in the lab had grown heavy, a physical weight that pressed against Elias’s temples. He stared at the main terminal, the blue light reflecting in his tired eyes, illuminating the dark circles that had become permanent fixtures under his gaze. For three days, the Meridian Project’s core encryption had been failing in subtle, almost imperceptible ways. A millisecond delay here, a packet loss there. Nothing that would trigger an alarm, but enough to make Elias’s stomach churn with a dread he couldn’t name. He had tried to dismiss it as a glitch, a temporary hiccup in the server farm’s cooling system, but the pattern was too deliberate. It felt like a whisper, a warning from the code itself. Thomas had been insistent on a full shutdown for a deep diagnostic, but Elias had refused. The board meeting was tomorrow. They couldn’t afford downtime. They couldn’t afford to look weak. So, they had compromised. They would run the diagnostics remotely, quietly, while the system remained live. It was a risk, but a calculated one. Elias rubbed his face, the rough skin of his palms scraping against his stubble. He needed to see Thomas. He needed to hear his voice, that steady, logical anchor that kept Elias from spiraling into paranoia. He grabbed his coat, the fabric cold against his skin, and checked his watch. 11:45 PM. Thomas would be in the penthouse office, likely already reviewing the logs. The elevator ride up would be short, a brief respite from the humming chaos of the server room. As he stepped into the elevator, the doors sliding shut with a metallic clank, Elias felt a sudden, sharp chill that had nothing to do with the air conditioning. He looked back at the floor number ticking upward, 40, 41, 42. The reflection in the polished steel doors showed a man who looked older than his years, haunted by secrets he hadn’t yet fully understood. He pressed the button for the top floor, the penthouse, where the city lights waited to blur into the rain."
}

