  "text": "The rain had started an hour ago, a relentless drumming against the high windows of the Meridian Tower that seemed to synchronize with the erratic blinking of the status lights on Elias’s monitor. He hadn’t moved from his chair since the initial anomaly report popped up on his screen, a small, red notification that had initially looked like a standard system error. But as the minutes ticked by, the errors multiplied, cascading through the core architecture like a virus spreading through a healthy body. Elias’s fingers hovered over the keyboard, trembling slightly, not from the cold, but from the sudden, visceral realization that this was not a malfunction. It was an intrusion. A sophisticated, patient, and deeply personal one. He remembered the last time he had seen Thomas, three days ago, in the sterile white conference room. Thomas had looked pale, his usual confident demeanor replaced by a hollow, fearful stare. \"We need to talk, Elias,\" Thomas had said, his voice barely above a whisper. \"Before the board meets. Before they find out what we’ve built.\" Elias had brushed it off, attributing Thomas’s anxiety to the pressure of the upcoming launch. He had been wrong. The code on his screen now pulsed with a rhythm that matched his own racing heart, a digital heartbeat that felt alive. He tried to trace the source of the intrusion, but the logs were scrubbed, clean as if they had never existed. It was as if the system was hiding something from him, or perhaps, protecting him. The air in the lab grew colder, the temperature dropping despite the servers running at full capacity. A low hum, almost subsonic, began to vibrate through the floor, a sound that seemed to come from the walls themselves. Elias stood up, his legs unsteady, and looked around the empty room. The shadows in the corners seemed to stretch, reaching toward him. He realized then that he was not alone. The silence was not empty; it was waiting."
